            body: `Intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) occurs when a defendant, by extreme and outrageous conduct, intentionally or recklessly causes severe emotional distress. The conduct must go beyond all bounds of decency and be regarded as atrocious and utterly intolerable in a civilised community. Insults, indignities and annoyances do not qualify. Courts look for abuse of a position of power, exploitation of a known vulnerability, or a pattern of persistent harassment. Severe distress is typically shown by physical manifestations or professional treatment, though the requirement varies. IIED is often a gap-filler for conduct that fits no other tort and is regarded warily by courts as a risk to free expression. Recklessness suffices, and where a defendant is a common carrier or an innkeeper it may be liable for gross insults.

The First Amendment constrains IIED when the speech concerns public figures or public issues. Hustler Magazine v. Falwell (US 1988) held that a public figure cannot recover for IIED based on a parody unless he shows a false statement of fact made with actual malice. Snyder v. Phelps (US 2011) held that members of a church picketing near a military funeral, with signs on public issues, were protected by the First Amendment from IIED liability, because the speech addressed matters of public concern and occurred on public property.

Trespass to land is an intentional physical invasion of land in the possession of another. The intent required is only to enter the land or to cause the object to enter, not to trespass. Mistaken belief that the land is one's own is no defense. Liability attaches without proof of harm, with nominal damages available. Invasion may be by a person, an object or even a substance thrown on the land, and subsurface and airspace invasions can qualify, though courts treat airplane overflights as trespass only if they interfere substantially with the use of the land. A person who stays beyond a licence or after a consent has been revoked becomes a trespasser. Possession, not ownership, supports the claim, so a tenant can sue a landlord.

Trespass to chattels is an intentional interference with another's personal property that either dispossesses the owner for a substantial time or damages the property or impairs its condition, quality or value. Mere touching without harm will not do. The remedy is damages for the diminution in value or loss of use. Courts have applied the tort, sometimes controversially, to electronic intrusions such as unauthorised flooding of servers, where the plaintiff shows actual impairment.

Conversion is an intentional exercise of dominion or control over chattel so serious that it justifies requiring the defendant to pay the property's full value. It is a more serious interference than trespass to chattels. Relevant factors include the extent and duration of the control, the defendant's good faith, the harm done and the inconvenience caused. Good faith is no defense: a person who buys stolen goods innocently and sells them converts them. The usual remedy is the value of the chattel at the time of conversion, and the plaintiff may alternatively recover the chattel itself through replevin. Taking a chattel by mistake, using it a bit and returning it will often amount only to trespass to chattels.

Worked example: A neighbour borrows a lawnmower for an hour, but keeps it for a month and rents it to others, so that the owner has to buy a new one. This is conversion: the extent, duration and use make it serious. A neighbour who uses it for five minutes without permission and returns it undamaged has not committed conversion or, absent impairment, trespass to chattels, though an owner might claim nominal damages for trespass to chattels in some jurisdictions.`
