            body: `A defendant who has committed the elements of an intentional tort may avoid liability by proving a privilege or defense. The main ones are consent, self-defense and defense of others, defense of property, necessity and legal authority.

Consent can be express, as in words or a signed form, or implied from conduct or custom, as when someone extends an arm for a vaccination or enters a contact sport. Consent is effective only within its scope. A participant in a football game consents to the contacts ordinary to the game but not to a deliberate punch after the whistle. Consent is ineffective if obtained by fraud about the essential character of the act, as when a person consents to a medical exam but the examiner has a sexual purpose. Consent may also be invalid because the person lacks capacity, because of duress, or because the conduct is criminal in a jurisdiction that bars consent to serious breaches of the peace. In medical settings, a doctor who goes beyond the scope of consent may commit a battery, though emergencies allow implied consent when a patient is unconscious and delay would endanger life.

Self-defense permits the use of reasonable force to prevent an imminent harmful or offensive contact, with the defendant's reasonable belief in the need for force sufficing even if mistaken. The force must be proportionate: deadly force is justified only against a threat of death or serious bodily harm. Many states impose a duty to retreat before using deadly force if retreat can be accomplished in complete safety, but a majority of states follow the no-retreat rule, and many have enacted stand-your-ground statutes. The defender cannot use force in response to words alone and loses the privilege after the threat ends. Defense of others follows similar rules, and most modern courts allow the intervenor to use force if a reasonable person would believe the third party was entitled to defend himself, even if mistaken.

Defense of property allows reasonable force to protect real or personal property from trespass or interference, but never deadly force solely to protect property. A person may use force only after a request to desist, unless a request would be futile or dangerous. Katko v. Briney (Iowa 1971) held that a landowner could not set a spring gun in an unoccupied farmhouse to guard against intruders and that he was liable for injury to a burglar, since the law places the value of human life above property. Reentry onto land and recapture of chattels have narrow privileges, and the shopkeeper's privilege permits reasonable detention.

Necessity allows the entry on or use of another's property to prevent a greater harm. Public necessity, as in destroying a house to stop the spread of fire, is a complete defense and no compensation is owed. Private necessity protects the actor from liability for trespass but not from liability for actual damage. In Ploof v. Putnam (Vermont 1908), a boater caught in a storm could dock at another's property without liability for trespass, and the owner had no right to unmoor the boat. In Vincent v. Lake Erie Transportation Co. (Minnesota 1910), the ship owner who kept his vessel tied to the dock during a storm was held liable for the damage caused to the dock, since private necessity does not extend to the harm done.

Worked example: A hiker, lost in a blizzard, breaks into an empty cabin and burns a few chairs for heat. Private necessity protects him from trespass liability, but he must pay for the chairs and for any damage done.`
