            body: `Negligence is conduct that falls below the standard of care that a reasonable person would exercise, causing harm. A plaintiff must prove four elements: duty, breach, causation (both actual and proximate), and damages. Duty asks whether the defendant owed the plaintiff an obligation of reasonable care. The general rule is that everyone owes a duty to avoid creating unreasonable risks of physical harm to others, and the Restatement (Third) frames the default as a duty of reasonable care whenever the actor's conduct creates a risk of physical harm.

The classic debate over the scope of the duty is Palsgraf v. Long Island Railroad Co. (New York Court of Appeals 1928). A railroad guard pushed a passenger who was carrying a package of fireworks, the package fell and exploded, and the shock knocked down scales that injured Mrs. Palsgraf, who was standing far away. Judge Cardozo, writing for the majority, held that duty runs only to foreseeable plaintiffs within the zone of danger, and Mrs. Palsgraf was not one because nothing suggested the package posed a danger to someone at that distance. Judge Andrews, dissenting, argued that anyone who breaches a duty of care owes it to the world at large and that the limit should be set by proximate cause. Cardozo's approach is the majority view of duty in many courts, while many others treat the question as proximate cause.

There is generally no affirmative duty to act. A person who sees a stranger drowning has no legal duty to rescue, even at no risk. Exceptions arise from special relationships, such as common carrier and passenger, innkeeper and guest, shopkeeper and customer, employer and employee, custodian and ward, and in some states school and student. A duty to act also arises where the defendant created the peril, whether innocently or negligently, where the defendant voluntarily begins a rescue and must then act with reasonable care and not leave the victim worse off, or where a statute or contract imposes one. Some states impose a limited statutory duty to report or give aid in specific circumstances.

Premises liability historically depended on the entrant's status. Owners owe trespassers no duty beyond refraining from wilful or wanton harm, though known trespassers and children attracted by an artificial condition (the attractive nuisance doctrine) can alter the analysis. Licensees, who enter with permission for their own purposes, are owed a warning of known concealed dangers, and invitees, who enter for the owner's business purposes, are owed reasonable inspection and the repair or warning of dangers. Many states have abolished the distinction between licensees and invitees, and a minority have abolished status entirely in favour of general reasonable care under all the circumstances.

Negligent infliction of emotional distress is a limited duty. Courts generally allow recovery by those within the zone of danger who fear for their own safety, and in many jurisdictions by bystanders who witness serious injury to a close relative, as in Dillon v. Legg (California 1968), which looked at proximity, contemporaneous perception and close relationship. Some states also allow recovery in special categories such as mishandling a corpse or erroneously informing a person of a death. Duty is a question of law for the court, and policy considerations such as administrability may eliminate a duty altogether.

Worked example: A hotel guest sees another guest unconscious by the pool and walks past. She owes no duty to rescue, since no special relationship exists. The hotel, by contrast, may owe a duty to guests in distress because of the innkeeper relationship.`
